; Prototype-style objects: an "object" is just a plist name (see
; SETPROP/GETPROP/PROPLIST) with a "prototype" property pointing at its
; parent. NEW sets that property; SEND looks a message up on the
; object's own plist, falling back through however many "prototype"
; links it takes to find one -- no classes, no new value type, just a
; naming convention on top of property lists that already existed.

TO animal_speak :self
  PRINT SENTENCE :self GETPROP :self "sound
END

NEW "animal "nothing   ; the root of this hierarchy has no prototype
SETPROP "animal "speak "animal_speak
SETPROP "animal "sound "generic

NEW "dog "animal        ; dog descends from animal
SETPROP "dog "sound "Woof

SEND "dog "speak []       ; dog Woof     -- dog's own "sound" wins
SEND "animal "speak []    ; animal generic

; Methods inherit through the chain; data fields don't -- GETPROP
; never walks "prototype" on its own, only SEND's own message lookup
; does. So a "puppy" with no "sound" of its own inherits dog's "speak"
; method (which dog itself inherited from animal), but that method's
; own GETPROP :self "sound" call still comes back empty for puppy.
NEW "puppy "dog
SEND "puppy "speak []     ; puppy        -- inherited method, but no sound of its own

; A method can take more than just :self -- SEND prepends the object
; automatically, then passes along whatever args followed the message,
; via an explicit arglist ([] above for zero of them). bin/logo's SEND
; is fixed at 3 arguments (obj, message, arglist) rather than the old
; tree-walking engine's "however many the resolved method takes" --
; the bytecode compiler builds the whole AST before any value exists
; to look a method's arity up with, so it can't know at parse time how
; many trailing tokens a SEND call owns; an explicit arglist sidesteps
; that the same way Smalltalk's perform:withArguments: does.
TO animal_greet :self :name
  PRINT SENTENCE :self SENTENCE "hi :name
END
SETPROP "animal "greet "animal_greet
SEND "dog "greet [alice]   ; dog hi alice

; SEND is also an operator, not just a command -- a method that OUTPUTs
; can have its value captured, the same way a plain procedure can when
; used as an operator (PRINT double 5).
TO animal_getname :self
  OUTPUT :self
END
SETPROP "animal "getname "animal_getname
PRINT SEND "dog "getname []   ; dog
MAKE "n SEND "dog "getname []
PRINT :n                      ; dog

; SEND "dog "fly [] would report "SEND: dog does not understand fly" --
; there's no such message anywhere in dog's prototype chain.
